Finding: {"title":"buildFallbackSummary / computeHoursFromStages assume duration is a plain number; no defense against non-numeric or negative durations from AI or client","severity":"LOW","area":"Data correctness / money-adjacent math (hours)","files":["src/server/routes/routes-ai-curriculum-builder.ts"],"detail":"`computeHoursFromStages` (lines 202-237) does `const dur = m.duration ?? 0;` with no `Number()` coercion or NaN/negative guard, unlike the analogous `computeHours` in routes-ai-curriculum-analyzer.ts (line 159: `Number(m.duration) || 0`) and routes-curriculum-export.ts (line 194, same pattern). If the AI-generated JSON ever returns `duration` as a string (e.g. `\"60\"`) or omits proper numeric typing, `flightMin += dur` will silently produce `NaN` propagating into `summary.hours`, which is displayed to users and used for FAR-compliance pass/fail determination (buildFallbackSummary lines 801-809).","impact":"A malformed AI response could silently corrupt the hours summary and FAR-compliance calculation (a domain-critical field for FAA-regulated training) with `NaN`, which downstream UI likely renders as blank or 'NaN' rather than a caught validation error.","recommendation":"Apply the same `Number(m.duration) || 0` coercion pattern used in the sibling analyzer/export computeHours functions to computeHoursFromStages for consistency and defensive parsing of AI output.","confidence":"medium"}
